Node.js 26.10.0 and 24.22.0 add` util.debounce()` and` util.throttle()` to the` node:util` module, so server code can debounce and rate-limit functions without installing lodash or throttle-debounce.


If you’ve written another` setTimeout` /` clearTimeout` wrapper for a file watcher, or added` lodash.debounce` to a service that otherwise uses nothing from lodash, you no longer need to. Both helpers are now part of the runtime.


This article covers the release lines, the two signatures and their options, server-side examples for each, and what you need to change when you remove lodash. It continues our series on[Node.js built-in APIs that replace npm packages](https://blog.openreplay.com/nodejs-built-in-apis-replace-npm/) .


## Key Takeaways


- ` util.debounce()` and` util.throttle()` are available in Node.js 26.10.0+ and 24.22.0+. Both came from nodejs/node PR #65899.
- Every call to a debounced function returns a Promise. All calls in one debounce window settle with the result of a single invocation of` fn` .
- ` util.throttle(fn, limit, interval)` lets at most` limit` invocations start per` interval` milliseconds, and it queues extra calls by default instead of dropping them.
- Calls dropped by` util.throttle()` reject with` ERR_THROTTLED` , and Node marks those rejections as handled.
- Neither function has a` trailing` or` maxWait` option, so code ported from lodash has to be adjusted to Node’s option set.


## Which Node.js Versions Ship util.debounce and util.throttle


Both functions first shipped in[Node.js 26.10.0](https://nodejs.org/en/blog/release/v26.10.0) , a Current release from 22 September 2026. They were backported to[Node.js 24.22.0 ‘Krypton’ LTS](https://nodejs.org/en/blog/release/v24.22.0) on 14 October 2026. Both changes come from[PR #65899 by James M Snell](https://github.com/nodejs/node/pull/65899) . The util docs show “Added in: v26.10.0” for each function, but the 24.22.0 release notes list both under Notable Changes. On the 24.x line, the minimum version is 24.22.0, so any later 24.x patch also has them.


If you publish a library that supports older Node versions, check that` util.debounce` exists before using it, or keep a fallback.


## What Is the Difference Between Debounce and Throttle?


In Node.js,` util.debounce()` runs` fn` once after calls stop arriving for` wait` milliseconds, while` util.throttle()` limits how many calls to` fn` can start in each` interval` . For the from-scratch mechanics, see[debouncing and throttling for performance](https://blog.openreplay.com/forever-functional-debouncing-and-throttling/) .


## How Does util.debounce Work in Node.js?


` util.debounce(fn, wait\[, options\])` waits until` wait` milliseconds have passed since the most recent call and then invokes` fn` . Every call made in that window returns a Promise that settles with the result of that one invocation. The[util.debounce reference](https://nodejs.org/api/util.html#utildebouncefn-wait-options) lists three options:


Option Default Effect


` leading`` false` When true, the first call in a window invokes` fn` immediately


` rejectOnCancel`` false` When true, superseded calls reject with an` AbortError`


` signal` none Aborting cancels pending calls and blocks future ones. An already-aborted signal makes` debounce()` throw


The returned function also has` cancel(\[reason\])` ,` flush()` ,` pending` ,` pendingCount` ,` ref()` and` unref()` .


> Requires Node.js ≥ 24.22.0 or ≥ 26.10.0.


```text
import   {   debounce   }   from   '  node:util  '  ;


const   save   =   debounce  (  async   (  value  )   =>   `  saved:  ${  value  }`  ,   100  );


const   calls   =   [  save  (  '  a  '  ),   save  (  '  b  '  ),   save  (  '  c  '  )];
console  .  log  (  save  .  pendingCount  );   // 3
console  .  log  (  await   Promise  .  all  (  calls  ));   // [ 'saved:c', 'saved:c', 'saved:c' ]
```


` fn` receives only the arguments from the most recent call. To collect every event in a burst, store them outside` fn` and read them when it runs. The file watcher below does this with a` Set` :


```text
import   {   watch   }   from   '  node:fs  '  ;
import   {   debounce   }   from   '  node:util  '  ;


const   changed   =   new   Set  ();


const   rebuild   =   debounce  (  async   ()   =>   {
const   batch   =   [  ...  changed  ];
changed  .  clear  ();
console  .  log  (  '  rebuilding for  '  ,   batch  );
return   batch  ;
},   200  ).  unref  ();


const   watcher   =   watch  (  '  ./src  '  , (  eventType  ,   filename  )   =>   {
changed  .  add  (  filename  );
rebuild  ().  catch  (  console  .  error  );
});


process  .  on  (  '  SIGINT  '  , ()   =>   {
rebuild  .  flush  ();
watcher  .  close  ();
});
```


Calling` unref()` on a debounced or throttled function lets the process exit while its timer is still pending. That suits file watchers and log flushers, which should never block shutdown.` unref()` returns the function, so you can chain it onto the` debounce()` call. On shutdown,` flush()` runs the final batch so it isn’t lost.


Unlike throttle’s` ERR_THROTTLED` rejections, debounce doesn’t mark every rejection as handled. Node’s source marks a call’s promise as handled once a later call supersedes it, but the most recent call’s promise is not. If` cancel()` or an aborted` signal` rejects that promise and nothing catches it, the process gets an unhandled rejection. For fire-and-forget calls like the watcher callback above, attach a` .catch` .


## How Does util.throttle Rate-Limit Calls?


` util.throttle(fn, limit, interval\[, options\])` lets at most` limit` invocations of` fn` start in each` interval` milliseconds.` limit` must be greater than 0. By default, calls over the limit are queued in arrival order instead of discarded. The[util.throttle reference](https://nodejs.org/api/util.html#utilthrottlefn-limit-interval-options) defines these options:


- ` concurrency` (default` Infinity` ): the maximum number of` fn` calls in flight at once. A slot is released when` fn` ’s return value settles.
- ` maxPending` (default` Infinity` ): the queue size cap. It has no effect in drop mode.
- ` overflow` (` 'queue'` by default, or` 'drop'` ).
- ` strict` (default` false` ).
- ` signal` : when aborted, pending and future calls reject with an` AbortError` .


Rate capacity is used up when` fn` starts, not when a call joins the queue. The function also exposes` cancel(\[reason\])` ,` hasImmediateCapacity()` ,` pending` ,` pendingCount` ,` activeCount` ,` ref()` and` unref()` .


```text
import   {   setTimeout   as   sleep   }   from   '  node:timers/promises  '  ;
import   {   throttle   }   from   '  node:util  '  ;


const   callApi   =   throttle  (  async   (  id  )   =>   {
await   sleep  (  50  );   // stand-in for a real request
return   {   id   };
},   5  ,   1_000  , {   concurrency  :   2  ,   maxPending  :   100   });


const   calls   =   Array  .  from  ({   length  :   12   }, (  _  ,   i  )   =>   callApi  (  i  ));
console  .  log  (  '  active:  '  ,   callApi  .  activeCount  ,   '  queued:  '  ,   callApi  .  pendingCount  );
console  .  log  (  await   Promise  .  all  (  calls  ));   // results in call order
```


By default` util.throttle()` counts invocations in fixed windows. That can let two groups of calls start close together at a window boundary. Setting` strict: true` enforces the limit across every rolling interval instead.


To run a function at most once per second and discard the extra calls instead of queueing them, use` throttle(fn, 1, 1000, { overflow: 'drop' })` :


```text
import   {   throttle   }   from   '  node:util  '  ;


const   ping   =   throttle  (  async   ()   =>   '  pong  '  ,   1  ,   1_000  , {   overflow  :   '  drop  '   });


const   results   =   await   Promise  .  allSettled  ([  ping  (),   ping  (),   ping  ()]);
for (  const   r   of   results  ) {
console  .  log  (  r  .  status   ===   '  fulfilled  '   ?   r  .  value   :   r  .  reason  .  code  );
}
// pong, ERR_THROTTLED, ERR_THROTTLED


if (  ping  .  hasImmediateCapacity  ())   ping  ();   // gate instead of catching
```


Calls that` util.throttle()` drops reject with an[ERR_THROTTLED error](https://nodejs.org/api/errors.html#err_throttled) . A call is dropped either because` overflow` is` 'drop'` or because the` maxPending` queue is full. Node marks these promises as handled, and dropped calls use no rate or concurrency capacity.` cancel()` clears queued calls and resets the window, but it doesn’t stop invocations that have already started.


## How Do You Replace lodash.debounce and lodash.throttle?


` util.debounce()` and` util.throttle()` can replace` lodash.debounce` and` lodash.throttle` in most server code, but they don’t use the same model as lodash. Node’s throttle limits how many calls can start per interval, and it queues the rest. Lodash’s throttle allows at most one call per` wait` . Port your code using only the[node:util option set](https://nodejs.org/api/util.html) :


Concern What` node:util` does What to change


Return value Every call returns a Promise Handle the Promise with` await` or` .catch`


Edge options Only` leading` ; there are no` trailing` or` maxWait` options Remove those keys and rethink any` maxWait` logic, for example with a throttle


Throttle model Allows` limit` call starts per` interval` and queues extras Use a` limit` of 1 plus` overflow: 'drop'` for skip-the-extras behavior


Manual control` cancel(\[reason\])` and` flush()` Call them directly on the wrapper


` this` Inside` fn` ,` this` is the wrapper Bind methods first


```text
// before: hand-rolled
let   timer  ;
const   saveLater   =   (  doc  )   =>   {
clearTimeout  (  timer  );
timer   =   setTimeout  (()   =>   store  .  save  (  doc  ),   500  );
};
```


```text
// after
import   {   debounce   }   from   '  node:util  '  ;


const   saveLater   =   debounce  (  store  .  save  .  bind  (  store  ),   500  );
saveLater  (  doc  ).  catch  (  console  .  error  );
// on shutdown: saveLater.flush(); or discard: saveLater.cancel();
```


## When to Keep lodash or a Custom Helper


Keep lodash, throttle-debounce or a hand-written helper instead of` node:util` in two cases:


- **Browser code.**` node:util` is a server-side module. Front-end bundles still need their own helper; see[React debounce strategies](https://blog.openreplay.com/optimizing-api-calls-react-debounce-strategies/) .
- **Node versions older than 24.22.0.** This includes earlier 24.x patches and every older release line. Projects that have to support those versions should keep the dependency or add a feature check.


## Conclusion


On Node.js 26.10.0+ and 24.22.0+, debouncing and rate limiting are part of` node:util` . Both functions return Promises and come with` cancel()` and` unref()` built in, and the debounced function adds` flush()` . As a next step, find where your server code uses` lodash.debounce` or` lodash.throttle` , and replace one file watcher or outbound client with the built-in. Bind any methods before wrapping them, and add a` .catch` to every call you don’t await.


## FAQs


Does util.debounce with leading: true run the function twice?


Only if more calls arrive during the window. With leading set to true, the first call in a debounce window invokes fn immediately. A second, trailing invocation happens only if the debounced function was called again before the wait period ended. A single isolated call therefore runs fn exactly once, on the leading edge, and there is no trailing option to turn off.


Does util.debounce wait for an async function to finish before starting a new window?


No. After a trailing invocation, util.debounce can open a new debounce window even if the promise returned by fn has not settled yet, so two runs of a slow async fn can overlap. To prevent overlapping runs, use util.throttle with concurrency set to 1. Its concurrency slot is released only when the return value of fn settles.


How do I feature-detect util.debounce without breaking on older Node.js versions?


Import the whole module and check the property: import util from 'node:util', then test typeof util.debounce === 'function' before you use it, and fall back to a userland helper if it is missing. In ESM, a named import of an export that does not exist fails with a SyntaxError when the module links, so the code never gets a chance to run the check.


What happens to queued util.throttle calls when its AbortSignal is aborted?


They reject. When the signal passed to util.throttle is aborted, pending and future calls reject with an AbortError, and the signal's reason is set as the error's cause. Invocations that have already started keep running. Unlike ERR_THROTTLED rejections, the docs do not say these AbortError rejections are marked as handled, so attach a catch handler to every call you do not await.


